-- title:
--   Even height of endomorphisms with invertible coordinate matrix
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $r$, and write $B = W(k)/(r)$ for the quotient of the Witt vectors $W(k)$ by the ideal generated by $r$. Let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from $\mathrm{Zp2}\,r = W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})$ to $W(k)$, and let $\Phi$ be a `FormalODModule` over $B$: a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $\Phi.F$ over $B$ together with an action of $W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})$ by endomorphisms and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi = [r]$ and $\varpi\circ[a] = [\mathrm{Frob}(a)]\circ\varpi$. Assume: $\Phi$ is special with respect to $\iota$ followed by reduction modulo $(r)$, i.e. the submodules `lieZero` and `lieOne` attached to that homomorphism are complementary and each invertible as a $B$-module; and $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the two-variable system $\Phi.\mathrm{act}(r)$ has kernel of degree $r^4$ in the sense recalled below. Let $E$ be an injective ring homomorphism from the centralizer, inside the endomorphism ring of $\Phi.F$, of the set consisting of all $\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}\,a$ together with $\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$, into $M_2(\mathbb{Q}_r)$, whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathbb{Z}_r)$: there is $m \in \mathbb{N}$ with $r^m M_2(\mathbb{Z}_r)$ contained in the image of $E$ and $r^m E(e)$ integral for every $e$. Let $e$ be an element of that centralizer and $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_r)$ with $E(e)$ equal to the matrix of $g$. The conclusion is that there exists $m' \in \mathbb{N}$ such that the power series tuple of $e$ has kernel of degree $r^{2m'}$: the algebra `KerAlgebra` of that tuple is a finite projective $B$-module, and for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $B \to \kappa$ the base-changed kernel algebra has $\kappa$-dimension $r^{2m'}$.
--
--   This is the isogeny criterion for special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules of height $4$: an $\mathcal{O}_D$-linear endomorphism whose coordinate matrix in $M_2(\mathbb{Q}_r)$ is invertible is an isogeny, and its height is even. Together with the complementary computation of the determinant valuation it identifies the height of such an endomorphism as twice $v_r(\det E(e))$, and it is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld analysis of the moduli of rigidified special formal modules and of Cartier quadruples.
